euidiag-perf.py — offline analysis of /euidiag rec recordings

In game:

    /euidiag rec start          one sample a second
    /euidiag rec start 0.1      ten a second (floor 0.05; a frame is the real
                                floor, since the timer runs on OnUpdate)
    /euidiag rec start 1 20000  and a bigger sample cap
    /euidiag rec start 1 mem    also sample memory (see below; off by default)
    ... play ...
    /euidiag rec stop
    /reload                     the client only writes SavedVariables on
                                /reload or logout — the file does not exist
                                until then

Choosing an interval. The default cap is derived from the interval to hold
about 45 minutes — a key plus the walk in — so `rec start 0.1` covers a long
dungeon without a second argument. Measured against a real 10000-sample run:

    INTERVAL   DEFAULT CAP   COVERS    ON DISK   LUA HEAP
    1s               10000   166 min     2.2 MB     7 MB
    0.2s             13500    45 min     2.7 MB    10 MB
    0.1s             27000    45 min     5.2 MB    19 MB
    0.05s            54000    45 min    10.4 MB    38 MB

Heap is the cost that bites, not disk: Lua grows a table's array part by
doubling, so a 25-value sample sits in 32 slots. It is also paid twice — once
while recording, and again at every login after, when the client reads the file
back. A second argument overrides the cap: `rec start 0.1 40000`.

Memory sampling is opt-in, and the reason is the recording itself. The reading
comes from UpdateAddOnMemoryUsage, which walks every installed addon; measured
with the client's own profiler on a full UI it took 102 ms -- about eight
frames. Running that every few seconds made the diagnostics addon the largest
single frame cost in every trace it took: a 97-second idle recording produced
four spikes of 115-131 ms, all of them this call, against a worst of 14 ms from
any other addon. A recorder that writes the biggest spike in the trace into its
own trace is measuring itself.

So `rec start` no longer samples memory. `rec start 1 mem` turns it back on for
leak hunting, where the GROWTH table is the answer being read and the hitch is
the price of it -- but read the Spikes section in that mode knowing the addon's
own rows are the walk. Recordings without memory simply have no GROWTH section;
nothing else changes.

A sample used to carry a memory and a PeakTime column per module as well, which
made it 67 values instead of 25 and pushed it into a 128-slot table. Measured
over a real 10000-sample run those 42 columns were 63% of the file and 1.2% of
the information: memory refreshes every few seconds however fast the sampler
runs, and PeakTime is a high-water mark that changed 5 times in 210,000 stored
values. Both now have their own table on their own cadence, so nothing is lost
and the same recording costs a quarter of what it did.

What finer sampling can and cannot buy is worth knowing first. The CPU columns
come from the client's RecentAverageTime, which is the average over the last 60
frames — about 0.6s at 90 fps. Two samples 0.1s apart therefore share most of
their frames, and no sample rate makes those columns resolve a single frame.
What does sharpen: frame rate, memory, and WHEN a spike arrives relative to a
pull. For single-frame cost, the Spikes section is the instrument — it reads
PeakTime, which is per-frame and does not average.

Then:

    ./euidiag-perf.py                      newest recording, summarised
    ./euidiag-perf.py --list               what is in the file
    ./euidiag-perf.py -r 2 --csv out.csv   export recording 2 (plus out-marks.csv)
    ./euidiag-perf.py --out report.txt     save the summary as printed
    ./euidiag-perf.py --share run.json     small JSON to hand to somebody else
    ./euidiag-perf.py --plot trace.png     needs matplotlib; skipped without it

With no path argument it finds the SavedVariables file itself: it locates the
game the same way the installer does (see wow_tools/wow.py) and takes the most
recently written recording it finds. Pass a path to read another account's or
another install's, or set $WOW_SAVEDVARIABLES to pin one. If the game lives
somewhere the search does not reach, $WOW_INSTALL points at it.

EllesmereUISecretsDiag is left out of every table and out of the plot. It only
runs because you asked for measurements — its cost is not something the suite
does to a normal session, and it is loud enough to crowd out the modules you
came to read. `--include-self` puts it back.

Other switches: `--top N` for how many modules each TABLE shows (default 15),
`--plot-top N` for how many lines the PLOT draws (default 8), `--no-marks` to
drop the mark list from the end of the summary.

Sharing a run

  `--share run.json` writes the whole analysis as a small JSON file: every
  statistic the summary prints, the encounters, the marks, the spikes, and one
  trace reduced to 240 points (`--share-points N`). Each bucket keeps a mean and
  a max, so a spike survives the reduction instead of being averaged away.

  A 10000-sample 0.1s recording came out at 35 KB, about 10 KB gzipped, against
  a 5.1 MB SavedVariables file.

  It does not carry the account path: the source is recorded as the file's name
  only, since the directory it lives in is the account number. The character and
  realm stay, because a recording without them cannot be reproduced.

The plot draws eight modules and sums the rest into one gray "other" line.
Eight is where a categorical palette stops being readable — past it, hues have
to repeat, and two identical blues on one chart are worse than an honest
aggregate. `--plot-top 14` overrides it and separates the repeats by dash
instead. Each module keeps the same hue between recordings, so two traces taken
before and after a change can be read side by side.

What the output means

  source               The SavedVariables file these numbers came from, its
                       size and when the client last wrote it. There is one per
                       account, and the tool picks the newest by itself, so the
                       summary and the PNG both name the one they used.
  Frame budget         The whole client frame, all addons together, and this
                       suite, side by side. A module's 0.08 ms means nothing
                       until you know the frame is 12 ms and every addon in it
                       adds up to 0.7 ms.
  MEAN vs P95/MAX      A module with a low mean and a high P95 is spiking, and
                       spikes are what get felt. `/euidiag cpu spikes` in game
                       shows the same thing as cumulative counters.
  In combat vs out     Split by the combat marks. A module that costs the same
                       standing still as it does mid-pull is doing work it does
                       not need to do; a high RATIO is a module behaving.
  Encounters           One row per boss: length, fps, and which module led.
  Spikes               The recorder stores PeakTime, which is a high-water mark
                       and never falls, so the raw number only reports what the
                       addon did while loading. This reports the STEPS UP during
                       the run instead — each one is a real worst-ever frame,
                       with the time it happened and the mark it happened under.
                       "none" is the good result.
  Worst moments        The lowest-fps samples, 10s apart so one dip is one row,
                       with the frame time, the addon total, and the loudest
                       module at that instant.
  GROWTH / DROPS       Memory at the end minus at the start, plus how many times
                       the reading fell. Growth with no drops is a leak; growth
                       with many drops is garbage the collector has not reached.
  Marks                Combat, encounter, keystone and zone boundaries, so a
                       spike in the trace can be attributed to something.

One caveat worth knowing before blaming a module: the engine bills a script
handler's entire call tree to the addon whose execution context created the
frame carrying the handler, not to the file the code lives in. Work handed to a
frame the parent created is billed to the parent. `Core (parent)` absorbing an
implausible share is usually that, not a parent-side regression.
EllesmereUI_Ticker.lua documents the rule and how to stay on the right side of it.
